Monday after a rest Sunday, the scale is often up. You did not “undo” the week. You stopped sweating, you maybe ate a bit more carbohydrate, and glycogen took water back into muscle. The jump is a recovery sign more often than a fat-gain sign.

## What changes when you do not train

Training costs water. You sweat. You breathe harder. Muscle stays a little inflamed and “pumped.” On a rest day those outputs fall. If you still eat and drink on a normal schedule, more of that fluid stays in the tank overnight.

Carbohydrate on a rest day is not wasted. It refills glycogen you emptied during the week. Glycogen binds water. The scale notices. Your next hard session needs that fuel.

## Other rest-day culprits

- **Restaurant or family meals.** Rest days are when people eat out. Sodium climbs. Water follows for 24–48 hours.
- **Alcohol.** It can dehydrate you the night of, then rebound the next morning as you rehydrate.
- **Sleeping in.** You weigh later, after extra water and maybe breakfast. That is a different protocol, not a different body.
- **Less NEAT.** No commute to the gym, more sitting. A smaller deficit for one day does not equal fat gain you can see on a scale.

## How to check if it is just a rest-day bump

Keep weekday weigh-ins. Note rest days in the same log as training. A 0.4–1.2 kg rise the morning after a day off, then a drift back by midweek, is a common pattern on a cut.

Worry about the trend if:

- The weekly average keeps rising
- Waist measurements drift up for a month
- Training loads stall because you feel heavy and under-recovered for reasons other than one rest day

One Tuesday reading is not a trend.

## What to do instead of extra cardio panic

Take the rest day. Walk if you like walking. Eat protein as usual. Weigh the next morning with the same rules. Open last week’s sessions and confirm you still hit the work.

If rest-day anxiety makes you add random HIIT, you often just add fatigue. Fatigue makes the next strength session worse. Worse sessions are a real cut problem. A 700 g water bump is not.

## Log rest days on purpose

A blank calendar looks like you “did nothing.” A logged rest day looks like a plan. Mark it. Keep the streak honest: training days are training, rest is rest. The scale will keep wobbling. Your log should not.
